From 80d3c4dde85c94bd145c84be85ed1c7ee05bd72a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shyalya <shyalya@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 12:46:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] A creature took damage and was handed to player-only code

Unit::DealDamage branched on

    if (!pVictim->IsPlayer() && addThreat)   { ...threat... }
    else                                     // "victim is a player"
    { RewardRage(); DurabilityPointLossForEquipSlot(); }

The comment is wrong. The else branch is reached whenever the victim is a
player OR addThreat is false, so any damage to a creature that carries no
threat fell into the player-only half and got cast to Player*.

Crash of 2026-08-08 at 11:40, an uncaught exception rather than a segfault:

  Object::GetUInt32Value            Object.h:447   (MANGOS_ASSERT throws)
  Player::DurabilityPointsLoss      Player.cpp:6158
  Player::DurabilityPointLossForEquipSlot
  Unit::DealDamage
  Aura::PeriodicTick

The same pointer, 0x7ff70e8e9750, appears as Player in frame 8 and as
Creature in frame 19 - the object was a creature all along, and the index
check tripped on whatever the cast produced.

Now the branch asks what the victim is first and whether threat applies
second. Players and creatures-with-threat behave exactly as before; only the
case that was misfiled goes where it belongs.

This is core code, not playerbots. Any tree with the same branch has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/game/Objects/Unit.cpp | 28 +++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/game/Objects/Unit.cpp b/src/game/Objects/Unit.cpp
index f39e2cc..107c129 100644
--- a/src/game/Objects/Unit.cpp
+++ b/src/game/Objects/Unit.cpp
@@ -1014,18 +1014,28 @@ uint32 Unit::DealDamage(Unit* pVictim, uint32 damage, CleanDamage const* cleanDa
                 pVictim->RemoveAurasWithInterruptFlags(AURA_INTERRUPT_FLAG_DIRECT_DAMAGE);
         }
 
-        if (!pVictim->IsPlayer() && addThreat)
-        {
-            float threat = damage * sSpellMgr.GetSpellThreatMultiplier(spellProto);
-
-            if (spell && !spell->m_addThreat)
+        // Split on what the victim is, and only then on whether threat applies.
+        // Written as "!IsPlayer() && addThreat", the else branch caught every
+        // creature hit without threat as well and handed it to the player-only
+        // code below, which casts pVictim to Player*. Crash of 2026-08-08: a
+        // periodic tick on a creature reached DurabilityPointLossForEquipSlot,
+        // and Object::GetUInt32Value threw on the resulting nonsense - the same
+        // pointer appeared as Player in one frame and Creature in another.
+        if (!pVictim->IsPlayer())
+        {
+            if (addThreat)
             {
-                // empty on purpose.
+                float threat = damage * sSpellMgr.GetSpellThreatMultiplier(spellProto);
+
+                if (spell && !spell->m_addThreat)
+                {
+                    // empty on purpose.
+                }
+                else
+                    pVictim->AddThreat(this, threat, (cleanDamage && cleanDamage->hitOutCome == MELEE_HIT_CRIT), damageSchoolMask, spellProto);
             }
-            else
-                pVictim->AddThreat(this, threat, (cleanDamage && cleanDamage->hitOutCome == MELEE_HIT_CRIT), damageSchoolMask, spellProto);
         }
-        else                                                // victim is a player
+        else                                                // victim really is a player
         {
             // Rage from damage received
             if (this != pVictim && pVictim->GetPowerType() == POWER_RAGE)
